    description: "## Origin\n\nMaterialized 2026-06-04 from the 2026-06-03 fold-audit (`~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/notebookjournal_06022026_2100_fold_audit_passes_21-40.md`, Family C). Stand-alone finding F24.6 previously folded onto WI-bupim. Full evidence remains on WI-bupim's pass-24 discussion entry; this row provides discrete representation as a structurally-distinct INV-bazij sub-mechanism.\n\nThe audit's verdict: F24.6 is structurally distinct from WI-bupim's typed-tier name-disambiguation scope. Bash export-VAR is within-name repetition at the (path, name, kind) triplet level — each `export VAR_NAME` statement at a different line emits a fresh Symbol node, so multiple declarations of the same variable in one file produce multiple symbols sharing all the disambiguation inputs the hash would use. WI-bupim is about hash-input sufficiency for distinct symbols; this row is about analyzer-level dedup choices for re-declared symbols.\n\n## Symptom\n\nThe bash analyzer treats each `export VAR_NAME` statement as a distinct Symbol node. For variables declared multiple times in one file (a common bash pattern), this produces multiple Symbol nodes sharing path + name + kind. Concrete from pass-24 F24.6 on the root substrate:\n\n| kind | nodes | unique (path,name,kind) triplets | dup rate |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **export** | **20** | **10** | **50.00%** |\n| call_site | various | — | ~40% |\n| other | ~32,615 | ~32,400 | 0.66% |\n\nFiltering out `external_symbol` kind (whose `<external>` path makes the triplet degenerate by design), 295/32,615 = 0.904% of nodes share (path, name, kind) with at least one other node — and the export-kind 50% rate dominates that residual.\n\nExample: `scripts/auto-pr` declares `CI_TIMEOUT_SECONDS` 3 times at different lines, producing 3 Symbol nodes for the same variable. Same pattern on `FORGEJO_TOKEN` (5×, top INV-bazij collision group on SCRIPTS substrate).\n\nPass-38 F38.B5 negative half: bash has **0 cross-name collisions** (unlike TypeScript). Different fix path from WI-typescript-hash-input-insufficient — bash needs per-statement-line disambiguation (or analyzer-level dedup) rather than additional hash inputs.\n\n## Cross-substrate scope\n\nThis cohort is the dominant driver of the SCRIPTS substrate's INV-bazij DEFAULT-mode breach at 7.0% (pass-38 F38.D3). Top groups on SCRIPTS DEFAULT:\n- FORGEJO_TOKEN 5×\n- CI_TIMEOUT_SECONDS 4×\n- usage 4×\n- all bash export-VAR cohort.\n\nINV-bazij threshold-calibration assumption (≥500 files / production scale; pass-37 F37.A1) doesn't hold for bash-heavy codebases of any size. Updated cross-substrate matrix (snapshot):\n\n| Codebase | Nodes | Rate | Dominant cohort |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| ROOT default | 34253 | 0.84% | mixed (this cohort masked at scale) |\n| TRACKER | 4959 | 0.81% | python residual |\n| **SCRIPTS default** | **416** | **7.0% BREACH** | **bash export-VAR (this row)** |\n| HTRAC default | 77 | 16.88% BREACH | TS hash-input (WI-typescript-hash-input-insufficient) |\n| CLI_PKG | 32 | 0.00% | — |\n| DOCS default | 2861 | 0.38% | markdown within-file-dup-heading (WI-sirid) |\n\n## Invariant\n\nThe bash analyzer should emit at most one Symbol node per (path, name, kind) triplet, even when the source contains multiple declarations of the same exported variable. The dedup choice has a behavioral question attached — see below.\n\n## Fix sketch (two paths)\n\n**(a) Analyzer-level dedup.** When `export VAR_NAME` is seen, check the existing per-file Symbol set; if a Symbol with the same (path, name, kind) exists, skip emission. Pro: matches the WI-bupim \"one Symbol per concept\" framing; cleanest from a downstream-consumer perspective.\n\n**(b) Per-statement-line disambiguation.** Include `line` in the stable_id hash for bash export-kind Symbols. Pro: preserves provenance of each individual declaration site; cleaner if downstream consumers care about each location.\n\nThe choice depends on whether re-export of the same variable is meaningfully a separate \"thing\" in the call graph or whether it's noise. The current substrate evidence shows the redundant Symbols don't add semantic information (same name, same shell scope, same export semantics) — argues for (a). But path (b) is mechanically identical to the existing hash-input extension pattern (parallels WI-typescript-hash-input-insufficient's planned fix), so it's lower-risk in terms of refactor scope.\n\nRecommendation: (a) for `export VAR_NAME`. Reassess for other within-name-repetition bash patterns if discovered.\n\n## Source provenance\n\n- **F24.6** — WI-bupim discussion entry 2026-06-02T04:54:12 (pass-24 batch; see paragraph beginning \"Pass-24 finding (F24.6) — `export` (50% dup rate) and `call_site` (40% dup rate)\").\n- F38.B5 (negative half: bash has 0 cross-name collisions, fixing the bash row independently from the TS row) — WI-bupim 2026-06-02T22:15:54.\n\nLab notebooks: passes 24, 38 under `~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/`.\n\n## Related rows\n\n- Parent: **INV-bazij** (META: stable_id mass collisions) — sibling under same parent to WI-bupim and WI-sirid.\n- Sibling: **WI-typescript-hash-input-insufficient** (Family C — different language, different mechanism: TS cross-name collapse; this row is bash within-name repetition. Both are INV-bazij DEFAULT-mode breaches but cross-name vs within-name has different fix paths.)\n- Sibling: **WI-bupim** (typed-tier name disambiguation — distinct mechanism even though the audit currently lists this row under WI-bupim's discussion).\n- Cousin row: WI-vodin (canonical id-format static check)."  # 9551
